<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bonn</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bonn is a city on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-rhine-westphalia">North Rhine-Westphalia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a>. Founded as a Roman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legion">legionary</a> fortress, it became the residence of the archbishop-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-elector">electors</a> of Cologne from the late 13th century, and Beethoven was born there in 1770. From 1949 to 1990 it was the seat of government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-germany">West Germany</a>, and it kept several federal ministries after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> became the capital.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bonn.jpg" alt="Bonn" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Matthias Zepper · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:General_view_over_bonn_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The new state chose <b>Bonn</b> as a provisional capital to emphasise that the two-state solution was temporary.</blockquote><a href="/empire/federal-republic-of-germany">Federal Republic of Germany · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>By 338 votes to 320, the parliament of reunified Germany chose Berlin over <b>Bonn</b> as the seat of government.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-20">20 June in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Charles the Simple, king of West Francia, and Henry the Fowler, king of East Francia, signed a &quot;pact of friendship&quot; aboard a ship in the middle of the Rhine near <b>Bonn</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-7">7 November in history</a></li><li><blockquote>After French armies occupied the left bank of the Rhine, local supporters of the Revolution in towns such as <b>Bonn</b>, Koblenz, Cologne and Aachen proclaimed a Cisrhenian Republic in September 1797, with the encouragement of General Lazare Hoche, who favoured a separate state allied to France.</blockquote><a href="/state/cisrhenian-republic">Cisrhenian Republic</a></li><li><blockquote>After long conflicts with the citizens and the archbishop's defeat at the Battle of Worringen in 1288, Cologne became in effect a free city, and the electors moved to <b>Bonn</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/electorate-of-cologne">Electorate of Cologne</a></li></ul></section>
